  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100888,
      "id": "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a",
      "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ce3c7f8e-ea94-50b6-8342-262cb8d70f93",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{3 y}{x},\\qquad x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 4 x^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 16 x^{2}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=- 4 x^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 4 x^{3}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-4\\cdot 3 x^{2} \\\\ &=- 12 x^{2} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - 16 x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=- 4 x^{3}$ and $y\\prime=- 12 x^{2}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - 12 x^{2} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\left(- 4 x^{3}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 12 x^{2} &=- 12 x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=- 4 x^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "af93a449-eabe-5704-b21d-12a94d023d61",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
